2 CONT
2 CONT António Galvão (c. 1490–1557), known in English as Antonio Galvano, was a Portuguese soldier, chronicler and administrator in the Maluku islands, and a Renaissance historian, the first to present a comprehensive report of all the 
2 CONC leading voyages and explorers up to 1550, either by Portuguese and by other nationalities. His works show a remarkable accuracy, especially the Treaty of Discovery published in Lisbon in 1563 and in English by Richard Hakluyt in 1
2 CONC 601. Life António Galvão was the son of Duarte Galvão, chief diplomat and chronicler to king Afonso V of Portugal.[1] In 1527 he sailed for Portuguese India where he became captain of the Maluku and governor of the fort of Ternate
2 CONC  from 1536 to 1540.[2] He is described in Chapter II of the Fifth Decade of Asia" as a respected governor, having sent a mission to Papua and receiving local embassies. He funded a seminar in Ternate, where he spent twelve thousan
2 CONC d cruzados from the inheritance he had received from his father, and was known especially for his integrity.[3] In 1540 he handed the government of the fortress to D. Jorge de Castro and returned to Portugal, where he learned he h
2 CONC ad have fallen into disgrace.[1] He lived the last years in anonymity and poverty, in the Royal Hospital, awaiting a pension. There he died and was buried in 1557. Works António Galvão left two manuscripts. One was the treaty of t
2 CONC he discoveries printed after his death, in 1563, in Lisbon by his friend Francisco de Sousa Tavares.[4] Based on numerous written sources and documents, the work presented for the first time a synthesis of all the discoveries made
2 CONC  by Portuguese and Spanish until 1550. Richard Hakluyt had it translated and published in English in 1601 as "The discoveries of the world - by Antonio Galvano".
2 CONT
2 CONT Tratado que compôs o nobre & notauel capitão Antonio Galuão, dos diuersos & desuayrados caminhos, por onde nos tempos passados a pimenta & especearia veyo da India às nossas partes, & assi de todos os descobrimentos antigos & mode
2 CONC rnos, que são feitos até a era de mil & quinhentos & cincoenta, published 1563 in Lisbon by João da Barreira. PDF of original 1563 edition from Biblioteca Nacional de Portugal Digital. (A 1731 edition under the slightly altered ti
2 CONC tle, Tratado dos Descobrimentos, antigos e modernos, feitos até a era de 1550 was published in Lisbon by Oficina Ferreiriana). Galvaão, António, Richard Hakluyt (2004) [1862]. The Discoveries of the World from Their First Original
2 CONC  Unto the Year of Our Lord 1555 -Issue 30 of Works issued by the Hakluyt Society. Kessinger Publishing. ISBN 0-7661-9022-6. Based on the 1601 English translation The second manuscript was a history of the Moluccas (Maluku) with th
2 CONC e title:
2 CONT
2 CONT Historia das Molucas, da natureza, e descubrimento daquellas terras divida em 10 livros. ("History of the Moluccas, nature and discovery of the lands divided into 10 books") This remained unpublished; the manuscript was passed by 
2 CONC Francisco de Sousa Tavares to the crown. It is referred to by 16th-century chroniclers João de Barros and João Baptista Lavanha. It is alleged that a good part of it was reproduced verbatim within Damião de Góis's 1566-67 Chronica
2 CONC  del rey D. Manuel, but all trace of the original Galvão manuscript disappeared after Góis's death.[5] In 1928, a document was found in the Archivo General de Indias in Seville with a history of the Maluku which was identified as 
2 CONC being part (perhaps an early draft) of António Galvão's lost document. It was published in a bilingual (Portuguese-English) edition in 1971, arranged by Hubert Jacobs, under the title A Treatise on the Moluccas (c. 1544), probably
2 CONC  the preliminary version of António Galvão's lost Historia das Moluccas (Rome: Jesuit Historical Society).
