1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Nunes Henriques Family from Galicia, Spain
2 CONT  c.1547 •The family crests above are of Spanish origin.  •The earliest forms of hereditary surnames in Spain were the patronymic surnames, which are derived from the father's given name, and metronymic surnames, which are derived 
2 CONC from the mother's given name. Nunes was a patronymic surname, created from the personal name Nuño, using the common patronymic suffix is "ez." Spelling variations of the Nunes family name include: Núñez, Nuñez, Núnez, Nunez, Nuño 
2 CONC and Nuno and of the Henriques family name include: Enríquez, Enriquez, Enrique, Enriques, Enríquiz, Enriquiz, Enrique, Henríquez, Henriquez and Henríquiz. First found in Castile, predominant among the Christian kingdoms of medieva
2 CONC l Spain.  •Galicia: an ancient province in the northwestern part of Spain; a barren, mountainous region where Jews settled sparsely in the eleventh century. There were Jewish communities at Allariz, Coruña, Orense, Monforte, Ponte
2 CONC vedra, Rivadavia, and Rivadeo, besides individual Jews scattered here and there. D. Menendez Gonzalez, a rich and powerful nobleman, received Jewish merchants, probably from Allariz, in his domain, not far from Orense, and when th
2 CONC ey were attacked by Arias Oduariz in 1044, he led an armed force against the latter, and recovered the silks and other goods that had been taken from the Jews. When John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, invaded Spain (1385), and Rivad
2 CONC avia was taken by Sir Thomas Percy, the English soldiers attacked the Jews, who were supposed to be rich, and plundered them, killing several. The ghetto, however, was not destroyed. 
2 CONT  Eighteen years before the expulsion, the Jews of Coruña, Betanzos, and Rivadeo paid an annual tax of 1,800 maravedis, and those of Orense, Monforte, and Rivadavia one of 2,000 maravedis. A rich Jew of Rompusa, a tawer, was baptiz
2 CONC ed in 1414, taking the name "Juan Esteban." His sons obtained seats in the Parliament.  (The Jewish Encyclopedia)
